  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Saturday)\nIssued at 1252 PM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nTuesday continues an upward trek in PW's to ~0.75\", but overall \nprecipitation chances trend lower thanks to an axis of high pressure \nridging building in from the east. While isolated to scattered \nshowers with a few thunderstorms will favor the western high \nterrain, most areas look to see another pleasant day with high \ntemperatures warming into the 60s to 70s, with near 80F across the \neastern plains. \n\nThe overall synoptic scale weather pattern over the southwest CONUS \nbecomes sharply amplified by Wednesday with a 557-560dm H5 closed \nlow digging down the CA coastline and the aforementioned ridge of \nhigh pressure standing up further over eastern NM and the TX/OK \nPanhandles. Consensus timing for this upcoming wet period is \nWednesday through Friday with a peak intensity period occurring \nThursday focused through the northwestern half of NM. There is good \nconsensus among models and their associated ensembles that the H5\nlow will open and come ashore SoCal Thursday with a vortmax \nrotating northeastward around the low's southeasterly flank over \nthe Desert Southwest bringing fast moving thunderstorms flowing \nSW-NE through AZ and western NM thru the day. Individual storms \nwill be capable of producing strong gusty winds, and locally short\nduration periods of heavy rainfall. The main vortmax and area of \nlarge scale forcing and ascent ejects quickly along the UT/CO \nborder Thursday night into Friday morning with remnant showers \ncontinuing over central and eastern NM. Main uncertainties \nregarding this event still lay with just how much atmospheric \ninstability there will be to increase rainfall production. There \nis fair agreement that PW's climb to the 0.90\" to 1.10\" range at \nABQ by Thursday, which would likely set daily records for mid-to-\nlate October. However, the latest run of the NBM shows a 0.50\" to \n2.00\" range in 24-hr rainfall amounts between Thursday and Friday \nmornings over western and central NM. Much of this disparity in \nrainfall amounts will in the end depend on how much instability \nthere will be, in turn likely dependent on mid- to-upper level \ncloud cover. Numerical model guidance continues to advertise a \nwell defined tap of mid-to-upper level moisture streaming \nnortheastward from the EPAC and over AZ/NM. This feature would \nlikely produce abundant cloud cover, limiting daytime heating and\nlowering instability parameters. Thus, the mid range solutions \nfor 0.25\" to 0.75\" of event total rainfall Wednesday night through\nFriday morning for most lower and middle elevation areas of \nwestern, central, and northern NM with higher amounts of 0.50\" to \n2.00\" in the mountains where orographic forcing will also be a \nfactor looks to be the most likely scenario. Locally higher \namounts will favor where thunderstorms repeatedly train over the \nsame locations Thursday favoring the northwestern quadrant of NM \nand perhaps further into the middle Rio Grande Valley from Socorro\nto ABQ to Santa Fe. Thus, the WPC has maintained a marginal \nexcessive rainfall risk for these areas Thursday.\n\nThereafter, forecast certainty tanks further with diverging \nsolutions ranging from a lagging upper low closing off over NM and \nAZ which would continue the wet pattern, and a more progressive \nsolution bringing drier conditions back from the west.",
